#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128960 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129145 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4fc09a0f-34d3-5ca9-9964-6cb00498bfa7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Factored Polynomial Shown in a Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "2f03be7e-0a77-5869-b591-6a8f8f3942de" :question/math-academy-id "e-5814" :question/problem "![](images/59/5970275bd70bcfd944c03abc8f60c2b159e3545dc876a0dd05bc23343b85bdec.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the equation of the polynomial above?\n\nI. $y=(x+2)(x+1)^2(x-2)$\n\nII. $y=(x+2)^3(x+1)^2(x-2)$\n\nIII. $y=(x+2)(x+1)^2(x-2)^3$" :question/worked-solution "First, we notice that the function has precisely three distinct roots, $x=-2,$ $x=-1,$ and $x=2.$\n\nBased on the graph, we can make the following conclusions about the multiplicities:\n\n- Since the graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x =-2,$ this root is a simple root (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n\n- Since the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x =-1,$ this root has even multiplicity.\n\n- Since the graph passes through $x = 2,$ this root has odd multiplicity, and since the graph is tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=2,$ the multiplicity must be greater than one.\n\nFrom the given options, the only possible equation is $y= (x+2)(x+1)^2(x-2)^3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128963 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129147 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d03ac24f-82a6-5af8-b1b8-9021e521d47a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Graph of a Factored Polynomial"}] :question/id #uuid "e158501f-0bac-5d16-956a-59da7f82620f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5818" :question/problem "![](images/7d/7dc5d6aa3a8afed8ebcf49b6d884eee8a443bc759367b624bc7a92cfae515863.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be the graph of $y = (x+2)(x+1)^2(x-2)?$" :question/worked-solution "Based on the factored equation, we can make the following conclusions about the graph:\n\n- Since the multiplicity of the roots $x=\\pm 2$ is $1,$ which is odd, the graph must cross the $x$-axis at these points. Moreover, because $x=\\pm 2$ are simple roots, the graph must *not* be tangent to the $x$-axis at these points.\n\n- Since the multiplicity of the root $x=-1$ is $2,$ which is even, the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at this point.\n\nOnly the graphs $\\textbf{I}$  and $\\textbf{III}$ satisfy these conditions.\n\nTo choose between these two graphs, we need to consider the end behavior. Since the leading coefficient is positive, as $x$  approaches $\\infty,$ $y$  approaches  $\\infty.$\n\nOnly graph $\\textbf{III}$ displays this end behavior. Therefore, the correct answer is **$\\textbf{III}$**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128972 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129154 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a66188f6-3228-59e9-9766-0f9bacbe8443" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Graph of a Given Polynomial"}] :question/id #uuid "6d363483-4ce5-5c17-8ab8-11bfcf8f9282" :question/math-academy-id "e-5888" :question/problem "![](images/d8/d8d6f70aed4d013bfcdd9060124ad003e3e1ed6ce70852e4f60d71cdd8d65cc4.png)\n\nThe graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $x = -1$ is a root of even multiplicity.\n\nII. $x=-3$ and $x=2$ are simple roots.\n\nIII. $p(x)\\to-\\infty$ as $x\\to-\\infty.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The root $x = -1$ has an even multiplicity because at $x = -1$ the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction.\n\n- Statement II is true. The roots $x = -3$ and $x=2$ are simple roots because the graph crosses the $x$-axis at the points, but the curve isn't tangent to the $x$-axis at these points.\n\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ moves left in the negative direction towards $-\\infty,$ the $y$ value moves up in the positive direction towards $\\infty.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129078 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129253 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d48e2a0-1a76-5eec-a98c-4a7eb16f70cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Multiplicity of a Root Given a Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "53aa15a0-8633-51b8-8731-9099eec94940" :question/math-academy-id "e-9941" :question/problem "![](images/26/2683c694582a7c2dbe1c95cce6af22ef941a1e77b54721151a935e81ec2b3b1a.png)\n\nA part of the graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is given above. Which of the following statements is true?\n\n- $x=-4$ is a multiple root of odd multiplicity\n\n- $x=-4$ is a multiple root of even multiplicity\n\n- $x=-4$ is a simple root" :question/worked-solution "| Multiplicity | Sketch | Sketch |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ \n\n (Simple Root) | ![](images/5a/5a0221f93c65a1eeb4503604a558a6da81c4c7af81c2c52cc35ecf3d189fe99f.png) | ![](images/d5/d556b4edf39181eebb7ec87c344d6f657a6875ecd2745405eaf5351ccaff6f96.png) |\n| $>1$ \n\n (Multiple Root) \n\n Even | ![](images/9d/9d14c2588da2ee9c9f5bbc32a70791146bed609ea0d6d5912aeaa2edab02e2d7.png) | ![](images/28/28ad9248a9a509e3a44937d0438fb2366f550a22360d01b9b6a29bcac41357e8.png) |\n| $>1$ \n\n (Multiple Root) \n\n Odd | ![](images/3a/3ae81378cb3c918dd5b7730519eb5a91e949f88eef9f03f4910f4ab8fb3cd5a9.png) | ![](images/95/9506a7ce4031f1f6244c0110c3235f66ea5652bb53548a032732869cf21f5997.png) |\n\nIt's possible to determine information regarding the multiplicities of the roots of a polynomial from its graph.\n\n- If the graph intersects the $x$-axis but is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ we have a *simple* root $x=a.$\n\n- If the graph is *tangent* to the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ we have a *multiple* root $x=a.$ Furthermore:\n\n- If the graph touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x=a,$ the multiplicity of a root $x=a$ is *even*.\n\n- If the graph crosses the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ the multiplicity of a root $x=a$ is *odd*.\n\nIn our case, the graph is *tangent* to the $x$-axis at $x=-4.$ So, $x=-4$ is a *multiple* root. \n\nFurthermore, the graph touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x=-4.$ So, $x=-4$ has *even* multiplicity.\n\nTherefore, $x=-4$ is a multiple root of even multiplicity."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247294 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129147 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d03ac24f-82a6-5af8-b1b8-9021e521d47a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Graph of a Factored Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9dc60b75-554d-58d0-b027-ef3b794160a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{III}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245720} {:answer/id #uuid "c7100d83-8d57-52e6-94e1-b855e81e9f6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{I}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246077} {:answer/id #uuid "ce812ba4-0a43-5778-955c-7bd1edb83e74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{II}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246159} {:answer/id #uuid "dbae5fd7-431f-5c98-a632-966ebf920961" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{IV}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246265} {:answer/id #uuid "e210e43b-42d1-5c93-91f6-fb894fd6ce1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the given graphs are correct" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246326}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7100d83-8d57-52e6-94e1-b855e81e9f6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{I}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246077} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2ce298d-c1f9-5147-8aa9-67f9aa805a1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246873}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e82163b8-d12f-5c46-89d0-bd29c13ba90d" :question/math-academy-id "q-112599" :question/problem "![](images/15/1586e286d2b07aac28a42f91c76318ae209c46c19e14e429c62f0770ada9393b.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be the graph of $y=-x(x+2)^{2}(x-2)^{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "Based on the factored equation, we can make the following conclusions about the graph:\n\n- Since the multiplicity of the root $x=-2$ is $2,$ which is even, the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at this point.\n\n- Since the multiplicity of the root $x=0$ is $1,$ which is odd, the graph must cross the $x$-axis at this point. Moreover, because $x=0$ is a simple root, the graph must *not* be tangent to the $x$-axis at this point.\n\n- Since the multiplicity of the root $x=2$ is $3,$ which is odd, the graph must cross the $x$-axis at this point. Moreover, because $x=2$ is a triple-root, the graph must be tangent to the $x$-axis at this point.\n\nOnly the graphs $\\text{I}$ and $\\text{IV}$ satisfy these conditions.\n\nTo choose between these two graphs, we need to consider the end behavior. Since the leading coefficient is negative, as $x$ approaches $\\infty ,$ $y$ approaches $-\\infty .$ Since only graph $\\text{I}$ displays this end behavior, the correct answer is $\\text{I}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272218 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129253 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d48e2a0-1a76-5eec-a98c-4a7eb16f70cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Multiplicity of a Root Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28447685-a868-59d7-96f4-0ce2fb174971" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ is not a root of $p(x)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269628} {:answer/id #uuid "53d72a95-8d67-5140-849d-e2cce670e79d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ is a multiple root of even multiplicity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270014} {:answer/id #uuid "5f05af8c-d430-56f0-b720-e792c1742d17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ is a multiple root of odd multiplicity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270111} {:answer/id #uuid "898e1d62-814a-51eb-9763-f9deedc64e52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ is a simple root of even multiplicity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270455} {:answer/id #uuid "dae9da23-1600-51a5-9da6-48f9beaf7748" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ is a simple root" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271186}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f05af8c-d430-56f0-b720-e792c1742d17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ is a multiple root of odd multiplicity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270111} :answer-field/id #uuid "7781a1fe-189d-5a1f-9409-87fd83393a2e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271721}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5436f5c3-7c2a-57f2-a4bf-36a5ff8554a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-141133" :question/problem "![](images/33/33bec46293f976bfb829e987cd3ce26fa5cc67310bc830fc8f46b7f0d57b3acf.png)\n\nA part of the graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is given above. Which of the following statements is true?" :question/worked-solution "| Multiplicity | Sketch | Sketch |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ \n\n (Simple Root) | ![](images/5a/5a0221f93c65a1eeb4503604a558a6da81c4c7af81c2c52cc35ecf3d189fe99f.png) | ![](images/d5/d556b4edf39181eebb7ec87c344d6f657a6875ecd2745405eaf5351ccaff6f96.png) |\n| $>1$ \n\n (Multiple Root) \n\n Even | ![](images/9d/9d14c2588da2ee9c9f5bbc32a70791146bed609ea0d6d5912aeaa2edab02e2d7.png) | ![](images/28/28ad9248a9a509e3a44937d0438fb2366f550a22360d01b9b6a29bcac41357e8.png) |\n| $>1$ \n\n (Multiple Root) \n\n Odd | ![](images/3a/3ae81378cb3c918dd5b7730519eb5a91e949f88eef9f03f4910f4ab8fb3cd5a9.png) | ![](images/95/9506a7ce4031f1f6244c0110c3235f66ea5652bb53548a032732869cf21f5997.png) |\n\nIt's possible to determine information regarding the multiplicities of the roots of a polynomial from its graph.\n\n- If the graph intersects the $x$-axis but is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ we have a *simple* root $x=a.$\n\n- If the graph is *tangent* to the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ we have a *multiple* root $x=a.$ Furthermore:\n\n- If the graph touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x=a,$ the multiplicity of a root $x=a$ is *even*.\n\n- If the graph crosses the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ the multiplicity of a root $x=a$ is *odd*.\n\nIn our case, the graph is *tangent* to the $x$-axis at $x=-1.$ So, $x=-1$ is a *multiple* root. \n\nFurthermore, the graph crosses the $x$-axis at $x=-1.$ So, $x=-1$ has *odd* multiplicity.\n\nTherefore, $x=-1$ is a multiple root of odd multiplicity."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566220 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129253 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d48e2a0-1a76-5eec-a98c-4a7eb16f70cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Multiplicity of a Root Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b07e4e3-0c2c-57b8-8a98-da804eea979d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=4$ is a simple root" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565773} {:answer/id #uuid "3f8f19cb-60d5-5ba2-bb9c-13be76b60564" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=4$ is not a root of $p(x)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565784} {:answer/id #uuid "6db44c5f-20d5-5b85-8ecb-368e4d5cfdd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=4$ is a simple root of even multiplicity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565848} {:answer/id #uuid "933a91c2-1408-